    'histoire.cta.body':              'Open your TFSA on Wealthsimple — free and no hidden fees.',
    'histoire.cta.btn':               'Open an account →',
    /* ── TFSA & RRSP ── */
    'celi.hero.breadcrumb':           'TFSA &amp; RRSP',
    'celi.hero.tag':                  'TFSA · RRSP · Strategy · Wealth',
    'celi.hero.title':                'How I use the TFSA and RRSP<br>to <em>build my wealth</em>',
    'celi.meta.readTime':             ' 7 min read',
    'celi.meta.tags':                 'TFSA · RRSP · Strategy',
    'celi.lead':                      '"It\'s not enough to just save money. You need to understand where to put it — and why. That\'s what took me years to learn."',
    'celi.p1':                        'Over time, I developed a savings strategy that is balanced, tax-efficient, and adapted to my long-term goals. It\'s not a perfect strategy — it\'s mine, and it evolves over the years.',
    'celi.h2.strategy':               'My 3-Step Strategy',
    'celi.step1.badge':               'Top priority',
    'celi.step1.title':               'Maximize the TFSA first',
    'celi.step1.body':                'The TFSA is one of the most powerful tools in Canada. All gains — interest, dividends, capital gains — grow tax-sheltered. And withdrawals? Completely tax-free, at any time. It\'s my number one priority each year: putting $7,000 into my TFSA in January.',
    'celi.step2.badge':               'Tax optimization',
    'celi.step2.title':               'Contribute to the RRSP next',
    'celi.step2.body':                'Once my TFSA is maxed, I turn to the RRSP. Each dollar contributed reduces my taxable income — which lowers the tax I pay now. The tax is simply deferred to retirement, when income is generally lower. That\'s a real advantage, especially when you\'re in a higher tax bracket.',
    'celi.step3.badge':               'Beyond the limits',
    'celi.step3.title':               'Non-registered account for the rest',
    'celi.step3.body':                'When my registered accounts are optimized, I invest the surplus in a non-registered brokerage account. These investments are taxable, but they allow me to keep growing my wealth beyond the government ceilings.',
    'celi.h2.vs':                     'TFSA vs RRSP — it\'s not one or the other',
    'celi.vs.p1':                     'Many people pit the TFSA against the RRSP as if you had to choose. In reality, <strong>both are complementary</strong>. Each has its strengths depending on your situation.',
    'celi.compare.celiTitle':         '<span>TFSA</span> — Flexibility',
    'celi.compare.celi1':             '100% tax-free gains',
    'celi.compare.celi2':             'Withdrawals at any time without penalty',
    'celi.compare.celi3':             'Contribution room recovered the following year',
    'celi.compare.celi4':             'Ideal for short- and long-term goals',
    'celi.compare.celi5':             '$7,000/year in 2026',
    'celi.compare.reerTitle':         '<span>RRSP</span> — Tax optimization',
    'celi.compare.reer1':             'Reduces taxable income now',
    'celi.compare.reer2':             'Tax deferred to retirement',
    'celi.compare.reer3':             'Powerful when income is higher',
    'celi.compare.reer4':             'Ideal for retirement planning',
    'celi.compare.reer5':             '18% of prior year\'s income',
    'celi.pullquote':                 '"What matters most is building the habit of investing regularly — even with modest amounts at first. Compound interest does the rest."',
    'celi.h2.myth':                   'The multiple accounts myth — debunked',
    'celi.myth.p1':                   'Here\'s something I realized from talking with people around me: <strong>many believe you can only have one TFSA or one RRSP.</strong> That\'s absolutely not true.',
    'celi.myth.boxTitle':             'Myth vs Reality',
    'celi.myth.mytheLabel':           ' Myth',
    'celi.myth.mytheText':            'I can only have one TFSA. Opening a second TFSA account is illegal or non-compliant.',
    'celi.myth.realiteLabel':         ' Reality',
    'celi.myth.realiteText':          'You can have as many TFSAs as you want at as many financial institutions as you want. What\'s limited is the total contribution amount — not the number of accounts.',
    'celi.myth.p2':                   'The government sets an annual contribution limit — $7,000 in 2026 — but this limit applies to <strong>all your TFSAs combined</strong>, regardless of how many accounts you have. The same principle applies to the RRSP.',
    'celi.highlight':                 'It is often <strong>strategic to diversify across financial institutions</strong>. Some accounts are better for low-fee ETFs, others for tech funds, others still for liquid savings. There\'s no legal issue with having multiple accounts.',
    'celi.h2.portfolio':              'My 3 Current TFSAs',
    'celi.portfolio.p1':              'Personally, I currently have three TFSAs spread across different institutions, each with a specific purpose.',
    'celi.port1.inst':                'Starting out',
    'celi.port1.desc':                'My first TFSA. Over time, I realized that the fees and options were less aligned with what I was looking for.',
    'celi.port2.inst':                'Main account',
    'celi.port2.desc':                'My primary account today. Minimal fees, simple interface, commission-free ETFs. QQC.F is invested here.',
    'celi.port3.inst':                'Growth',
    'celi.port3.desc':                'Tech fund (ATL496). Performance of +48% at one point. A great lesson in sector diversification.',
    'celi.h2.learned':                'What I Really Learned',
    'celi.learned.p1':                'Investing isn\'t just about opening an account and letting your money sit. You need to understand the tools available, compare fees, diversify your holdings, and above all <strong>develop a strategy consistent with your personal financial goals</strong>.',
    'celi.learned.p2':                'Management fees, in particular, can have a huge impact on long-term returns. The difference between 0.20% and 2.5% in annual fees can represent tens of thousands of dollars over 20 years. It\'s a calculation every investor should do at least once.',
    'celi.disclaimer':                ' I am not a financial advisor. This article shares my personal experience. Consult a professional for your specific situation.',
    'celi.sidebar.title':             'Open Your TFSA',
    'celi.sidebar.body':              'Wealthsimple — $0 commission, simple interface, ETFs available.',
    'celi.sidebar.statsTitle':        'Key Numbers 2026',
