    'fu.hero.breadcrumb':   'Strategies',
    'fu.hero.tag':          'Savings · Emergency Fund · Interest',
    'fu.hero.title':        'Where to keep your<br><em>emergency fund?</em><br>What I learned',
    'fu.meta.readTime':     ' 5 min read',
    'fu.meta.tags':         'Savings · Liquidity · Interest',
    'fu.lead':              '"I understood something important: an emergency fund and investments are two completely different things. And mixing them up is the mistake many people make."',
    'fu.p1':                'When you start getting interested in personal finance, you hear a lot about investing, TFSA, ETFs. But there\'s an even more fundamental step that people often forget — or get wrong: <strong>keeping your emergency fund separate from your investments.</strong>',
    'fu.h2.buckets':        'Two very different things',
    'fu.bucket1.title':     'Emergency fund',
    'fu.bucket1.desc':      'Money that\'s quickly accessible. For unexpected expenses, income gaps, emergencies. It shouldn\'t move — but it can still earn interest.',
    'fu.bucket1.tag':       'Accessible',
    'fu.bucket2.title':     'Investments',
    'fu.bucket2.desc':      'Money working long-term in the markets. You need to mentally consider it as non-existent — don\'t touch it, even when things go down.',
    'fu.bucket2.tag':       'Untouchable',
    'fu.pullquote':         '"For compound interest to truly work, invested money must stay invested. That\'s why you need a separate cushion — so you\'re never forced to withdraw at the wrong moment."',
    'fu.h2.howMuch':        'How much to put in your emergency fund?',
    'fu.p2':                'The general rule: between <strong>3 and 6 months of expenses</strong>. For entrepreneurs or self-employed workers, aim for 6 to 12 months — because income is irregular.',
    'fu.highlight':         'Once that amount is reached → stop adding to it. The rest goes directly into investments. <strong>The goal isn\'t to accumulate cash — it\'s to have a safety net.</strong> No more, no less.',
    'fu.h2.where':          'Where to keep it — and put that money to work',
    'fu.p3':                'Your emergency fund doesn\'t have to sit idle. It can earn interest in a high-interest savings account — no risk, no markets, always accessible.',
    'fu.th.institution':    'Institution',
